{"author":{"id":"a3a282966ced55effb4560271fcd98e13241af24ea76b00ec76a0f18154bc8ee"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1790976225,"nonce":"wQK9C6h/AVZxc4Ps3iU3th1txrg=","message":"2026-10-02 (Luis): `depth` goes away; `expand` takes a recursive spec. A layer is an object: `relation` (the stored or inverse name, required), and the list's own arguments for that layer — `query` (over the row's candidate children, every unarchived one by default, so done children show unless the layer says otherwise), `fields`, `details`, `rank`, `group_by` — and an optional `expand` for the layer below; none means the layer's rows are leaves. A string stays the shorthand for one layer with defaults (`\"expand\": \"children\"`). `\"expand\": \"self\"` on a layer repeats it until nothing nests, cycles cut as today. The root query selects roots only; a root that is also another root's child is still shown once, under its parent. Per-layer `fields`: a layer's rows draw their own columns in their own widths under the parent, and the header describes the layer of the currently selected row, changing as Tab and S-Tab move between layers. The gantt takes the same spec (its start/stop/label/progress stay the chart's). The design doc's Nesting section is rewritten for this, and `view --help` describes a layer's keys.","files":null}]}